WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.010
In the world of digital 3D modeling, it often
occurs that we are looking for surfaces that

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.130
are not perfectly smooth, but have some sort
of surface detail.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:20.160
Wrinkles, engravings, grain on a wooden table
are excellent examples of details that we

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.050
can add to our models, and computer graphics
people like to collectively call these things

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.130
displacement maps.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.919
Artists often encounter cases where they like
the displacements on one object, but the object

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:34.480
itself is not really interesting.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.850
However, it could be that there is a different
piece of geometry these details would look

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:39.960
great on.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:46.060
Consider this problem solved, because in this
piece of work, the input is two 3D models:

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:51.360
one with interesting geometric details, and
the other is the model onto which we transfer

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.100
these surface details.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.830
The output will be our 3D geometric shape
with two of these models fused together.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.610
The results look absolutely amazing.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.729
I would love to use this right away in several
projects.

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:08.250
The first key part is the usage of metric
learning.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.920
Wait, technical term, so what does this mean
exactly?

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.079
Metric learning is a classical technique in
the field of machine learning, where we're

00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:21.670
trying to learn distances between things where
distance is mathematically ill-defined.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:26.249
Let's make it even simpler and go with an
example: for instance, we have a database

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:31.770
of human faces, and we would like to search
for faces that are similar to a given input.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.880
To do this, we specify a few distances by
hand, for instance, we could say that a person

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.780
with a beard is a short distance from one
with a moustache, and a larger distance from

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.830
one with no facial hair.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:48.409
If we hand many examples of these distances
to a learning algorithm, it will be able to

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:51.100
find people with similar beards.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.979
And in this work, this metric learning is
used to learn the relationship between objects

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:59.469
with and without these rich surface details.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:01.770
This helps in the transferring process.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.990
As to creating the new displacements on the
new model, there are several hurdles to overcome.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.740
One, we cannot just grab the displacements
and shove them onto a different model, because

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.060
it can potentially look different, have different
curvatures and sizes.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.000
The solution to this would be capturing the
statistical properties of the surface details

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.200
and use this information to synthesize new
ones on the target model.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.780
Note that we cannot just perform this texture
synthesis in 2D like we do for images, because

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:36.570
as we project the result to a 3D model, it
introduces severe distortions to the displacement

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.570
patterns.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:41.980
It is a bit like putting a rubber blanket
onto a complicated object.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.150
Different regions of the blanket will be distorted
differently.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.200
Make sure to have a look at the paper where
the authors present quite a few more results

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.610
and of course, the intricacies of this technique
are also described in detail.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:58.350
I hope some public implementations of this
method will appear soon, I would be quite

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.681
excited to use this right away, and I am sure
there are many artists who would love to create

00:03:03.681 --> 00:03:09.010
these wonderfully detailed models for the
animated films and computer games of the future.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:14.290
In the meantime, we have a completely overhauled
software and hardware pipeline to create these

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:15.290
videos.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.460
We have written down our joyful and perilous
story of it on Patreon - if you're interested

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.710
in looking a bit behind the curtain as to
how these episodes are made, make sure to

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.370
have a look, it is available in the video
description.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

